e(597123,
  '<div class="box-note">\n<p class="boxtitle">In the Lab</p>\n<p><strong>Experiment 7 &ndash; Amines and Amides:</strong> classify amines (1&deg;, 2&deg;, 3&deg;, quaternary) with models, test their water solubility and basicity (litmus), and convert amines to more soluble ammonium salts with acid. A cloudy, insoluble amine that clears when you add dilute HCl is the salt-forming reaction of this section happening in your test tube.</p>\n</div>',
  '<div class="box-note">\n<p class="boxtitle">In the Lab</p>\n<p><strong>Experiment 7 &ndash; Amines and Amides:</strong> build models of amines and classify them (1&deg;, 2&deg;, 3&deg;), test the odor, water solubility, and basicity (pH paper) of aniline, <em>N</em>-methylaniline, and triethylamine, and convert the amines to more soluble, odorless ammonium salts with acid. A cloudy, insoluble amine that clears when you add 10% HCl is the salt-forming reaction of this section happening in your test tube.</p>\n<p style="font-size:0.95em;"><strong>Lab manual:</strong> <a href="https://itsdrdan.github.io/chem3b-lab-manual/pdf/experiments/Exp07_AminesAmides.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Experiment 7 &ndash; Amines and Amides (PDF)</a></p>\n</div>',
  '7.3 In the Lab: Experiment 7 Parts A-C; basicity by pH paper',
  'exp07.js A.1-A.3 models (ammonia, methyl-, dimethyl-, trimethylamine, aniline, C3H9N isomers); B.1-B.4 odor, solubility, pH paper; C.1 10% HCl until blue litmus turns red. Litmus is used only to confirm enough HCl, not to test basicity.')

e(597127,
  '<div class="box-note">\n<p class="boxtitle">In the Lab</p>\n<p><strong>Amide hydrolysis:</strong> heating an amide with NaOH releases ammonia (or an amine). You detect it by its odor and by moist red litmus turning blue, which is the base-hydrolysis row of the table above happening in your test tube: the nitrogen leaves as the free base, not as a salt.</p>\n</div>',
  '<div class="box-note">\n<p class="boxtitle">In the Lab</p>\n<p><strong>Amide hydrolysis (Experiment 7 &ndash; Amines and Amides):</strong> heating an amide with NaOH releases ammonia (or an amine). You detect it by its odor and by moist red litmus held above the tube turning blue, which is the base-hydrolysis row of the table above happening in your test tube: the nitrogen leaves as the free base, not as a salt. When you heat the same amides with HCl, the ammonia is trapped as NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>, so you smell no ammonia.</p>\n<p style="font-size:0.95em;"><strong>Lab manual:</strong> <a href="https://itsdrdan.github.io/chem3b-lab-manual/pdf/experiments/Exp07_AminesAmides.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Experiment 7 &ndash; Amines and Amides (PDF)</a></p>\n</div>',
  '7.7 In the Lab: Experiment 7 Part D named; acid tube added',
  'exp07.js D.4 acetamide/benzamide + 10% HCl (acid hydrolysis, no ammonia odor) and D.5 + 10% NaOH with moist red litmus held above the tube.')

e(597137,
  '<p><strong>From Haworth back to Fischer:</strong> rotate the ring 90&deg; counterclockwise in your mind so that C1 is at the top, then read each position: up in the Haworth becomes left in the Fischer, down becomes right. The CH<sub>2</sub>OH tells you D (was up) or L (was down), and the anomeric OH tells you &alpha; or &beta;. Practice: a galactose ring drawn with the CH<sub>2</sub>OH up and the anomeric OH up is ___-D-galactose or ___-L-galactose? CH<sub>2</sub>OH up means D; anomeric OH up, same side as the CH<sub>2</sub>OH, means &beta;.</p>',
  '<p><strong>From Haworth back to Fischer:</strong> rotate the ring 90&deg; counterclockwise in your mind so that C1 is at the top, then read each position: up in the Haworth becomes left in the Fischer, down becomes right. The CH<sub>2</sub>OH tells you D (was up) or L (was down), and the anomeric OH tells you &alpha; or &beta;. Practice: a galactose ring drawn with the CH<sub>2</sub>OH up and the anomeric OH up is ___-D-galactose or ___-L-galactose? CH<sub>2</sub>OH up means D; anomeric OH up, same side as the CH<sub>2</sub>OH, means &beta;.</p>\n<div class="box-note">\n<p class="boxtitle">In the Lab</p>\n<p>In the Types of Carbohydrates workshop you build models of D- and L-glyceraldehyde to see that they are mirror images, then draw the Fischer projections of D-glucose, D-galactose, and D-fructose and turn them into Haworth rings with the same rules as this section. You label the anomeric carbon and draw both the &alpha; and &beta; anomers of glucose. Later parts of the workshop draw maltose, lactose, sucrose, and the glucose polymers of Sections 8.6 and 8.7.</p>\n<p style="font-size:0.95em;"><strong>Lab manual:</strong> <a href="https://itsdrdan.github.io/chem3b-lab-manual/pdf/experiments/Workshop_Carbohydrates.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Workshop &ndash; Types of Carbohydrates (PDF)</a></p>\n</div>',
  '8.4 NEW In the Lab box: Types of Carbohydrates workshop (after Fischer-to-Haworth section)',
  'workshop_carbohydrates.js A.1 D/L-glyceraldehyde models; A.2 Fischer of D-glucose then alpha and beta Haworth, label anomeric carbon; A.3 alpha-D-galactose and alpha-D-fructose; Parts B and C draw maltose, lactose, sucrose, amylose, amylopectin, cellulose.')

e(597138,
  '<div class="box-note">\n<p class="boxtitle">In the Lab</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Experiment 8a &ndash; Types of Carbohydrates</strong> and <strong>8b &ndash; Tests for Carbohydrates</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Benedict&rsquo;s test:</strong> reducing sugars turn blue Cu<sup>2+</sup> into a green, gold, or brick-red Cu<sub>2</sub>O precipitate (more sugar = redder). Ketoses also react because fructose rearranges to an aldose in base. Sucrose is negative.</li>\n<li><strong>Seliwanoff&rsquo;s test:</strong> ketoses (fructose) give a deep red color quickly; aldoses give only a light pink slowly.</li>\n<li><strong>Fermentation:</strong> yeast ferments most mono- and disaccharides (CO<sub>2</sub> bubbles), but not lactose (galactose ferments slowly, if at all).</li>\n<li>You will hydrolyze di- and polysaccharides, test foods, and identify an unknown sugar; the iodine test for starch is in Section 8.7.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>',
  '<div class="box-note">\n<p class="boxtitle">In the Lab</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Experiment 8 &ndash; Tests for Carbohydrates</strong> (the structures behind these tests are drawn in the Types of Carbohydrates workshop; see Section 8.4)</li>\n<li><strong>Benedict&rsquo;s test:</strong> reducing sugars turn blue Cu<sup>2+</sup> into a green, gold, or brick-red Cu<sub>2</sub>O precipitate (more sugar = redder). Ketoses also react because fructose rearranges to an aldose in base. Sucrose is negative.</li>\n<li><strong>Seliwanoff&rsquo;s test:</strong> ketoses (fructose) give a deep red color quickly; aldoses give only a light pink slowly.</li>\n<li><strong>Fermentation:</strong> yeast ferments glucose, fructose, sucrose, and maltose (CO<sub>2</sub> gas collects in the closed arm of a fermentation tube), but not lactose or starch (galactose ferments slowly, if at all).</li>\n<li>You will hydrolyze sucrose and starch with acid, test a food, and identify an unknown sugar; the iodine test for starch is in Section 8.7.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style="font-size:0.95em;"><strong>Lab manual:</strong> <a href="https://itsdrdan.github.io/chem3b-lab-manual/pdf/experiments/Exp08_TestsForCarbohydrates.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Experiment 8 &ndash; Tests for Carbohydrates (PDF)</a></p>\n</div>',
  '8.5 In the Lab: old 8a/8b replaced by Experiment 8; fermentation and hydrolysis matched to manual',
  'exp08.js Parts A-F: fermentation (glucose, fructose, sucrose, lactose, starch, unknown), Benedict, Seliwanoff, iodine, Part E acid (10% HCl) hydrolysis of sucrose and starch, Part F food test; unknown identified in D.3. Structures are in the separate workshop.')

e(597140,
  '<div class="box-note">\n<p class="boxtitle">In the Lab</p>\n<p><strong>Iodine test:</strong> amylose (starch) traps iodine in its helix and turns blue-black; as you hydrolyze starch with acid or amylase the color fades (and Benedict\'s test turns positive), which is how you follow the hydrolysis of a polysaccharide in Experiment 8b.</p>\n</div>',
  '<div class="box-note">\n<p class="boxtitle">In the Lab</p>\n<p><strong>Iodine test:</strong> amylose (starch) traps iodine in its helix and turns blue-black; amylopectin and glycogen, with only short coiled sections, give a red-purple to red-brown color. As you hydrolyze starch by heating it with HCl the blue-black color fades (and, once the acid is neutralized, Benedict\'s test turns positive), which is how you follow the hydrolysis of a polysaccharide in Experiment 8 &ndash; Tests for Carbohydrates. A starch tube heated with water instead of acid is the control.</p>\n<p style="font-size:0.95em;"><strong>Lab manual:</strong> <a href="https://itsdrdan.github.io/chem3b-lab-manual/pdf/experiments/Exp08_TestsForCarbohydrates.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Experiment 8 &ndash; Tests for Carbohydrates (PDF)</a></p>\n</div>',
  '8.7 In the Lab: Experiment 8b to Experiment 8; acid-only hydrolysis',
  'exp08.js Part E: starch and sucrose heated 10 min with 20 drops 10% HCl or water (control), neutralized with NaOH, then iodine and Benedict tests. Amylase appears only as background (digestion), not as a procedure. Part D gives red-purple/red-brown for amylopectin and glycogen.')

e(597145,
  '<div class="box-note">\n<p class="boxtitle">In the Lab</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Experiment 9 &ndash; Lipids.</strong> Lipids dissolve in nonpolar solvents but not in water.</li>\n<li><strong>Bromine test for unsaturation:</strong> Br<sub>2</sub> adds across each C=C in a fat or oil, so the more red color an oil decolorizes, the more unsaturated it is. Vegetable oils use up more bromine than solid animal fats.</li>\n<li><strong>Saponification:</strong> heating a fat with NaOH gives soap; you will test the soap\'s sudsing in soft and hard water and compare it with a synthetic detergent.</li>\n<li><strong>Hand lotion:</strong> you will make a lotion from stearic acid, cetyl alcohol, glycerol, and triethanolamine, and explain the role of each component.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>',
  '<div class="box-note">\n<p class="boxtitle">In the Lab</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Experiment 9 &ndash; Lipids.</strong> Lipids dissolve in nonpolar solvents such as methylene chloride but not in water.</li>\n<li><strong>Bromine test for unsaturation:</strong> Br<sub>2</sub> adds across each C=C in a fat or oil, so the more drops of red-orange bromine solution a sample decolorizes, the more unsaturated it is. Oleic acid, olive oil, and safflower oil take up bromine; saturated stearic acid does not.</li>\n<li><strong>Hand lotion:</strong> you will make a lotion from stearic acid, cetyl alcohol, lanolin, glycerol, and triethanolamine, see what changes when you leave out triethanolamine or stearic acid, and explain the role of each component.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style="font-size:0.95em;"><strong>Lab manual:</strong> <a href="https://itsdrdan.github.io/chem3b-lab-manual/pdf/experiments/Exp09_Lipids.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Experiment 9 &ndash; Lipids (PDF)</a></p>\n</div>',
  '9.2 In the Lab: Experiment 9 Lipids, soap/detergent removed',
  'exp09.js Part A solubility in water vs CH2Cl2; Part B bromine drops for oleic acid, olive oil, safflower oil vs stearic acid; Part C lotion (stearic acid, cetyl alcohol, lanolin, triethanolamine, glycerol, ethanol, water) with C.2 no TEA and C.3 no stearic acid. No saponification, soap, or detergent part.')
